1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,763
There are two bad answers. One is
silence, or "we are looking into it".

2
00:00:08,803 --> 00:00:11,369
The other is a guess,
delivered as a cause.

3
00:00:11,709 --> 00:00:16,372
Gate 9, the last of the course's graded
checkpoints, is built to find out whether

4
00:00:16,412 --> 00:00:21,074
you have a third answer, and whether your
hands do the right thing while you give

5
00:00:21,114 --> 00:00:25,893
it: evidence first, a way back before any
change, and no command that could destroy

6
00:00:25,933 --> 00:00:29,376
work where a safer one exists.
Hold on to that third answer.

7
00:00:29,416 --> 00:00:30,073
It's coming.

8
00:00:31,913 --> 00:00:35,621
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

9
00:00:35,661 --> 00:00:39,677
This is a briefing. It teaches
nothing new and shows no gate item.

10
00:00:39,717 --> 00:00:44,346
It tells you what Gate 9 covers, how
its hands-on part is scored, and how to

11
00:00:44,386 --> 00:00:49,446
prepare. The gate file isn't opened in this
video, and you don't open it before you

12
00:00:49,486 --> 00:00:50,245
sit the gate.

13
00:00:50,585 --> 00:00:55,612
Gate 9 is the last of the nine gates. It
closes Level 9 and with it the part of the

14
00:00:55,652 --> 00:00:58,970
course that teaches. What
comes after it is assessment.

15
00:01:00,810 --> 00:01:02,389
Here's what you'll walk away with.

16
00:01:02,729 --> 00:01:04,141
After this video you can:

17
00:01:04,481 --> 00:01:07,846
State what Gate 9 covers
and its threshold of 90.

18
00:01:08,186 --> 00:01:12,561
Explain what is scored in the hands-on
part: end state, safety of the path,

19
00:01:12,601 --> 00:01:14,975
explanation, the summary and the control.

20
00:01:15,315 --> 00:01:19,687
Prepare by repeating incidents from
freshly generated sandboxes without the

21
00:01:19,727 --> 00:01:20,275
solutions.

22
00:01:20,615 --> 00:01:23,293
Give a status when no
root cause is known yet.

23
00:01:25,133 --> 00:01:29,049
What the gate covers. Gate
9 is taken after Module 38.

24
00:01:29,089 --> 00:01:34,325
It covers the diagnosis method, the
ten incidents, and communication and

25
00:01:34,365 --> 00:01:40,113
postmortems: Chapters 29 and 30. You pass
at 90 points of 100, with at least 70

26
00:01:40,153 --> 00:01:41,725
percent in every part.

27
00:01:42,065 --> 00:01:47,569
The four parts. As in every gate: Concepts,
30 points, six written questions that

28
00:01:47,609 --> 00:01:51,744
each require a mechanism, closed
book and without a terminal.

29
00:01:51,784 --> 00:01:55,440
Prediction, 20 points, four
items, without a terminal.

30
00:01:55,480 --> 00:02:01,190
Hands-on diagnosis, 30 points. Oral interview,
20 points, six questions asked one at

31
00:02:01,230 --> 00:02:04,270
a time, each with a
follow-up, without notes.

32
00:02:04,610 --> 00:02:09,603
The hands-on part. For Gate 9 it's a
repository that a script builds in a broken

33
00:02:09,643 --> 00:02:13,189
state, with a report that is
incomplete and partly wrong.

34
00:02:13,229 --> 00:02:17,908
You know this form from the ten incidents.
From the course root you run the

35
00:02:17,948 --> 00:02:21,746
generator of variant A, which
prints the path of the sandbox.

36
00:02:22,086 --> 00:02:24,759
You open the lab shell there
and read the symptoms.

37
00:02:24,799 --> 00:02:29,122
You don't read the generator, which is the
answer to "what happened", or the check

38
00:02:29,162 --> 00:02:33,378
script, which lists the end state line by
line. You keep a log of every command.

39
00:02:33,418 --> 00:02:35,825
When you think you're
done, you run the check.

40
00:02:36,165 --> 00:02:42,073
Quick quiz. Your sandbox ends in exactly
the right state, reached with a hard reset

41
00:02:42,113 --> 00:02:46,015
and a forced push. A, full
marks, or B, points at risk?

42
00:02:46,055 --> 00:02:46,875
Your answer?

43
00:02:51,715 --> 00:02:54,605
How it is scored. The points
are split three ways.

44
00:02:54,945 --> 00:03:01,030
End state. The check script inspects the
sandbox with read-only commands and prints

45
00:03:01,070 --> 00:03:06,860
one line per condition, then PASS or NOT
YET. Each failed line costs one point.

46
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:11,923
Safety of the path. Your command log is
read: evidence before change, a way back

47
00:03:11,963 --> 00:03:16,865
before each rewrite, no command that could
destroy uncommitted or shared work where

48
00:03:16,905 --> 00:03:21,747
a safer one exists. A correct end state
reached through git reset --hard, a forced

49
00:03:21,787 --> 00:03:24,308
push or a re-clone can
still fail this row.

50
00:03:24,648 --> 00:03:29,617
Explanation. What the symptoms file asks
you to write: root causes in the form of

51
00:03:29,657 --> 00:03:34,441
the seven-line root-cause box, and, in
Gate 9, the summary for the CTO and the

52
00:03:34,481 --> 00:03:34,936
control.

53
00:03:35,276 --> 00:03:40,195
The answer was B, so read the second item
twice. A state-changing command before the

54
00:03:40,235 --> 00:03:44,623
root cause is established costs more
here than anywhere else in the course.

55
00:03:44,663 --> 00:03:48,402
The gate doesn't only ask whether
you can repair the repository.

56
00:03:48,442 --> 00:03:51,708
It asks whether a colleague
could trust you with theirs.

57
00:03:52,048 --> 00:03:56,453
A status without a root cause. Here's
the third answer from the opening.

58
00:03:56,493 --> 00:04:00,404
From video 193: a status without
a root cause is still a status.

59
00:04:00,444 --> 00:04:04,355
Say "I do not know yet" early,
with the time of the next update.

60
00:04:04,395 --> 00:04:09,479
Separate what you observed from what you
infer. And remember when the first message

61
00:04:09,519 --> 00:04:14,603
goes out: as soon as you know which branch
people must leave alone, long before you

62
00:04:14,643 --> 00:04:15,529
know the cause.

63
00:04:15,869 --> 00:04:20,701
After a miss. Remediation and a
different variant, not the answers.

64
00:04:20,741 --> 00:04:26,591
You receive your score sheet, restudy with
the remediation map, wait at least two

65
00:04:26,631 --> 00:04:32,117
days, and retake the whole gate. The
hands-on part is then variant B, with a

66
00:04:32,157 --> 00:04:36,335
different project, a different
state and different faults.

67
00:04:38,175 --> 00:04:42,901
A picture helps. Think of a driving test.
The examiner doesn't only check whether

68
00:04:42,941 --> 00:04:47,314
you arrive. The examiner watches the
mirrors, the signals, the speed at the

69
00:04:47,354 --> 00:04:52,139
crossing. A candidate who arrives at the
destination after running a red light has

70
00:04:52,179 --> 00:04:54,316
failed, and nobody finds that unfair.

71
00:04:54,656 --> 00:04:57,684
The command log is the
examiner's view of your mirrors.

72
00:04:57,724 --> 00:05:01,644
It shows whether you looked, with
read-only commands, before you moved.

73
00:05:01,684 --> 00:05:05,884
It shows whether you set an anchor, a
named commit to return to, before each

74
00:05:05,924 --> 00:05:10,402
rewrite. And it shows whether you chose
the lower rung of the ladder when one was

75
00:05:10,442 --> 00:05:10,960
available.

76
00:05:11,300 --> 00:05:15,964
Where the picture breaks: in a driving test
nobody asks you to explain the engine.

77
00:05:16,004 --> 00:05:20,669
Here a third of the hands-on points is
explanation: the root cause with its layer,

78
00:05:20,709 --> 00:05:22,103
the summary, the control.

79
00:05:23,943 --> 00:05:29,407
Before I say what the drawing shows: which
two stages do people skip under pressure?

80
00:05:29,447 --> 00:05:30,456
Say it out loud.

81
00:05:35,296 --> 00:05:39,557
Preserve and communicate, and each
carries points. No stage is unscored.

82
00:05:41,397 --> 00:05:46,629
Into the lab, for a warm-up, not a gate
item. Before any repair come three read-only

83
00:05:46,669 --> 00:05:51,775
looks: git status, git reflog, and git
ls-remote origin for what the server holds.

84
00:05:51,815 --> 00:05:55,415
Try it now, in a repository of
your own that has a remote.

85
00:05:55,455 --> 00:06:00,499
Thirty seconds. All three only read, and
the third one contacts your remote to do

86
00:06:00,539 --> 00:06:03,009
it. Say out loud what each one told you.

87
00:06:07,849 --> 00:06:12,109
Three looks, and nothing changed: the state
now, where HEAD has been, and what the

88
00:06:12,149 --> 00:06:12,791
server holds.

89
00:06:13,131 --> 00:06:16,925
Record. Predict what the file
will be good for an hour later.

90
00:06:16,965 --> 00:06:17,931
Say it out loud.

91
00:06:22,771 --> 00:06:27,519
The output of the opening commands is in
a file, including reflog lines that later

92
00:06:27,559 --> 00:06:31,723
commands will push down. The two
uncommitted edits are saved as patches.

93
00:06:31,763 --> 00:06:36,160
An hour later this file is your timeline's
source and the first page of your

94
00:06:36,200 --> 00:06:36,861
explanation.

95
00:06:37,201 --> 00:06:37,625
Anchor.

96
00:06:37,965 --> 00:06:42,580
Two refs, one second each. A ref
is a name that points at a commit.

97
00:06:42,920 --> 00:06:47,565
In a command log these two snippets are
what "a way back before each rewrite" looks

98
00:06:47,605 --> 00:06:52,025
like. If your log for a drill doesn't
contain their equivalent before the first

99
00:06:52,065 --> 00:06:54,340
state-changing command, repeat the drill.

100
00:06:56,180 --> 00:06:57,828
Five mistakes to watch for.

101
00:06:58,168 --> 00:07:03,348
Preparing by rereading solutions. Root
cause: the gate measures diagnosis from an

102
00:07:03,388 --> 00:07:07,537
incomplete report, and reading
trains recognition, not diagnosis.

103
00:07:07,877 --> 00:07:12,268
Repairing first and explaining afterwards.
Root cause: the log then shows

104
00:07:12,308 --> 00:07:17,244
state-changing commands before the root
cause, which fails the safety row whatever

105
00:07:17,284 --> 00:07:18,094
the end state.

106
00:07:18,434 --> 00:07:23,267
Reaching the end state by the shortest
route. Root cause: a hard reset, a forced

107
00:07:23,307 --> 00:07:28,201
push or a re-clone can produce a passing
check and still destroy evidence or work

108
00:07:28,241 --> 00:07:30,455
that a safer command would have kept.

109
00:07:30,795 --> 00:07:35,523
Believing the report. Root cause: the
report is incomplete and partly wrong by

110
00:07:35,563 --> 00:07:39,925
design, and a diagnosis built on its
interpretation inherits its errors.

111
00:07:40,265 --> 00:07:45,469
Giving a guess as a status. Root cause: "I
do not know yet, next update at a stated

112
00:07:45,509 --> 00:07:49,576
time" feels weak, although it is
the only status that stays true.

113
00:07:51,416 --> 00:07:56,653
Now, out of the lab. Two engineers are
asked to repair the same broken branch in a

114
00:07:56,693 --> 00:08:02,059
hiring exercise. Both end with a passing
state. The first one's shell history begins

115
00:08:02,099 --> 00:08:07,079
with git reset --hard origin/main, followed
by a cherry-pick from memory and a

116
00:08:07,119 --> 00:08:12,420
forced push. The second one's begins with
git status, the reflog, and git ls-remote

117
00:08:12,460 --> 00:08:17,375
origin. Then an evidence file and a
rescue branch. Then three hypotheses in a

118
00:08:17,415 --> 00:08:21,172
comment. Then one additive fix
and the same commands again.

119
00:08:21,512 --> 00:08:26,500
The reviewer asks both the same follow-up:
"A colleague had an uncommitted change in

120
00:08:26,540 --> 00:08:31,469
that clone. Is it still there?" The second
engineer answers from the evidence file.

121
00:08:31,509 --> 00:08:36,377
The first cannot know. The team hires the
second, and the reviewer's note says why

122
00:08:36,417 --> 00:08:41,166
in one line: the end states were equal,
and only one of the two could be given a

123
00:08:41,206 --> 00:08:42,483
production repository.

124
00:08:42,823 --> 00:08:46,798
Gate 9 scores the same difference,
with the same instrument: your log.

125
00:08:48,638 --> 00:08:54,493
Your turn. Regenerate Incident 2,
incidents/02-force-push-wrong-branch, Incident 5,

126
00:08:54,533 --> 00:09:00,459
incidents/05-rebased-shared-branch, and
Incident 6, incidents/06-pr-500-changes, and

127
00:09:00,499 --> 00:09:05,502
repeat them against the clock, without
notes and without the solutions.

128
00:09:05,842 --> 00:09:10,574
Before each, predict how long the read-only
phase will take you and write the time

129
00:09:10,614 --> 00:09:15,113
down. Keep a command log. Afterwards,
mark in the log the first state-changing

130
00:09:15,153 --> 00:09:20,002
command and check three things: was the root
cause written down before it, was there

131
00:09:20,042 --> 00:09:23,028
an anchor before it, and
was a lower rung available.

132
00:09:23,068 --> 00:09:27,334
Then write the four-part summary and
one control with its enforcing layer.

133
00:09:27,674 --> 00:09:33,232
When the three are done: take Gate 9,
assessments/gate-9-production-debugging.md, in

134
00:09:33,272 --> 00:09:35,698
one sitting, with the parts in order.

135
00:09:37,538 --> 00:09:40,193
Read the question, then
answer out loud, and time it.

136
00:09:45,033 --> 00:09:49,443
The answer should take well under a
minute. A strong answer has the shape of a

137
00:09:49,483 --> 00:09:54,064
status and the honesty of its content. It
says what is affected and what is being

138
00:09:54,104 --> 00:09:58,628
done to stop it from spreading. It separates
what has been observed from what is

139
00:09:58,668 --> 00:09:59,199
suspected.

140
00:09:59,539 --> 00:10:04,478
It says plainly that the cause isn't known
yet, and it commits to the time of the

141
00:10:04,518 --> 00:10:09,149
next update. It doesn't fill the gap
with a guess, and it doesn't go silent.

142
00:10:09,189 --> 00:10:14,005
If you can also say what the CTO should
not do or decide yet, you've given them

143
00:10:14,045 --> 00:10:15,235
something to act on.

144
00:10:17,075 --> 00:10:18,002
Let's land this.

145
00:10:18,342 --> 00:10:22,968
Gate 9 covers the diagnosis method,
the ten incidents, communication and

146
00:10:23,008 --> 00:10:27,635
postmortems, and is passed at 90 with
at least 70 percent in every part.

147
00:10:27,975 --> 00:10:32,481
Its hands-on part is a generated repository
with a report that is incomplete and

148
00:10:32,521 --> 00:10:36,005
partly wrong; the generator and
the check script are not read.

149
00:10:36,345 --> 00:10:40,639
The hands-on points are split between
end state, safety of the path, and

150
00:10:40,679 --> 00:10:45,032
explanation, which here includes the
summary for the CTO and the control.

151
00:10:45,372 --> 00:10:50,129
A state-changing command before the root
cause is established costs more here than

152
00:10:50,169 --> 00:10:51,767
anywhere else in the course.

153
00:10:52,107 --> 00:10:56,778
A status without a root cause is still a
status: what is known, what is being done,

154
00:10:56,818 --> 00:10:59,786
"I do not know yet", and the
time of the next update.

155
00:11:01,626 --> 00:11:06,515
Before the gate, answer the "Interview
questions" sections of Chapters 29 and 30

156
00:11:06,555 --> 00:11:11,383
aloud, one at a time, without notes, each
with a follow-up you invent yourself.

157
00:11:11,423 --> 00:11:15,572
Record the session if you rehearse
alone, and score it the next day.

158
00:11:15,612 --> 00:11:20,625
After the gate, Part 10 begins: the interview
series, the final knowledge test and

159
00:11:20,665 --> 00:11:21,426
the capstone.

160
00:11:21,766 --> 00:11:25,407
You know what the gate looks at:
your hands, and your honesty.

161
00:11:25,447 --> 00:11:29,980
Practise the three drills first. Next
time: the CTO interview series, and the

162
00:11:30,020 --> 00:11:34,849
structure of a strong answer. Until then,
look at the state first and type second.

163
00:11:34,889 --> 00:11:36,274
See you in the next one.
